    "reply": "Cystic fibrosis is linked to several specific genes identified in genetic records [2]. A key associated locus is NCBIGene:1080 [1]. Genetic associations also include NCBIGene:2212 and NCBIGene:7040 [3][4].\n\n## Associated Genes\n\nThe primary causal locus is NCBIGene:1080, known by the name CF transmembrane conductance regulator [1]. In addition, the condition is associated with NCBIGene:2212, which carries the name Fc gamma receptor IIa [3]. Another associated locus is NCBIGene:7040, designated by the name transforming growth factor beta 1 [4].\n\n## Clinical Manifestations\n\nPatients diagnosed with Cystic fibrosis exhibit widespread phenotypic effects across organ systems [2]. Documented clinical features in medical genetics include Bronchiectasis and Asthma [15][11]. Patients can also present with Clubbing of fingers, Dehydration, and Diarrhea [19][23][27]. Additional manifestations include Hemoptysis, Hepatomegaly, and Hepatosplenomegaly [28][29][30].\n\n## Hepatic Complications\n\nHepatic disease affects many individuals with this disorder, although brain dysfunction remains an uncommon outcome [26: \"CFLD) affects a large proportion of cystic fibrosis (CF) patients; however encephalopathy is a rare complication\"]. A rapid rise in ammonia levels causing brain dysfunction may be triggered in patients with established liver disease by systemic steroid treatments [26: \"hyperammonemic encephalopathy\\\" can be precipitated in previously stable CFLD by various triggers including systemic corticosteroids\"]. Severe end-stage liver involvement can also typically present with hepatic encephalopathy [26: \"classical hepatic encephalopathy can be a feature of end-stage liver disease\"].",
    "dur": 4.845
   },
