    "openrouter_cost_usd": 0.01080975,
    "reply": "Genetic associations for the disease Cystic fibrosis [2] include CF transmembrane conductance regulator [1], Fc gamma receptor IIa [3], and transforming growth factor beta 1 [4]. Supporting context identifies the condition under medgen title Cystic fibrosis [6] and literature entity Cystic Fibrosis [7].\n\n## Clinical Manifestations\nCystic fibrosis liver disease affects many individuals, yet encephalopathy remains an uncommon outcome [26: \"encephalopathy is a rare complication\"]. A documented clinical feature is Asthma [11]. Another clinical feature is Bronchiectasis [15]. Documented features also include Clubbing of fingers [19] and Dehydration [23]. Additional recognized manifestations include Diarrhea [27], Hemoptysis [28], Hepatomegaly [29], and Hepatosplenomegaly [30].\n\n## Literature Evidence\nResearch includes the publication Encephalopathy in cystic fibrosis [5]. Additional studies include 3.22 Nutrition in Cystic Fibrosis [10] and Editorial: Cystic Fibrosis in Children [14]. Further published work includes Azithromycin and cystic fibrosis [18] and Editorial: Cystic fibrosis-related diabetes [22]. Relevant indexed literature includes publication 32800451 [9], publication 35240644 [13], publication 35592838 [17], publication 35853636 [21], and publication 39129918 [25].\n\n## Clinical Studies\nA relevant registered study is Assessment of Quadriceps Muscle Electrostimulation Used in Patients Suffering From Cystic Fibrosis [8]. Another identified trial is Pregnancies and Cystic Fibrosis [24]. Related clinical evaluations include A Study of Topical Danazol for the Treatment of Pain Associated With Fibrocystic Breast Disease [12]. Other registered studies include Efficacy and Safety of ONCOXIN in Patients With Breast Fibrocystic Disease [16] and A Clinical Study on the Efficacy and Safety of the Treatment of Hyperplasia of Mammary Glands With Xiaoru Sanjie Capsule [20].",
    "dur": 4.815
